1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
This chapter walks through one fix in the sample file.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:12,000
In the sample, the first check is SHORT by $21.99.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:19,500
Check Detail shows why: four bills land on that one check.

4
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:29,000
Pick the fix that fits. Write it on the Short-Check Fix sheet in the PDF.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:38,500
Here the sample bills have been typed onto Bills, the way you would type yours. The car payment is due on the 8th.

6
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:46,000
Once a new date is confirmed, change the due day on Bills. Do not edit the Sample tab.

7
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,500
The first check now shows Covered. The car payment moved to the next check, which has room.

8
00:00:54,500 --> 00:01:02,000
The leftover chart now shows the first check above zero. A later short bar is still there to work on.

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:09,500
Check the next 6 checks too. A fix should not create a new SHORT.

10
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:17,000
The heads-up line now points to the next short check to work on.

11
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:25,500
Before a big purchase, type a price in the quick check on the Dashboard, or open Can I Afford It.

12
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:34,000
The answer is YES, YES using your cushion, NOT YET with the first check where it fits, or NOT IN THIS PLAN.

13
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Next chapter: debt payoff.
